I think you can have `struct Snapshot` and define
```
struct SnapshotList {
    struct Snapshot entries[MAX_MESSAGES_SNAPSHOT];
    size_t numEntries
};
```

Then in the code you have
```
struct SnapshotList lists[2];
struct SnapshotList *curr = &list[0];
struct SnapshotList *next = &list[1];
```
you access the current and next snapshot list by pointer and you `std::swap` them when needed. This should make the code a easier to read. For example, instead of having `snapshots[next][snapshotLens[next]].hdr` you have `next->entries[next->numEntries].hdr`

Instead of checking for header consistency, which will force us to update `VOICE_PROMPTS_DATA_NUMBER` every time a new prompt entry is added, I'd move the check into [`vp_queuePrompt`]( https://github.com/OpenRTX/OpenRTX/blob/1b31ba1b08b5d732ead34fa5d22d49202e5710e6/openrtx/src/core/voicePrompts.c#L385).
The idea is:
```
void vp_queuePrompt(const uint16_t prompt)
{
    if (prompt >= header->numPrompts)
        return;

    [...]
}
```

In theory it should work, but needs to be tested. Since you already did most of the work, I prefer you add this change yourself and retain the authorship of the patch.

