feedback.body:
About the VFO channel: as I mentioned a couple of times in the last days, I think that recycling a CPS channel for it is not the best way to go. Even more if you take into account that the current CPS implementation has major design flaws and needs to be redone (I wouldn't have pushed for PTSCD, otherwise).

What I had in mind for the VFO is something similar:
```
struct fmCfg {
    uint8_t rxTone;          //< RX CTC/DCS tone index
    uint8_t txTone;          //< TX CTC/DCS tone index
    uint8_t rxToneEn  : 1,   //< RX CTC/DCS tone enable
            txToneEn  : 1,   //< TX CTC/DCS tone enable
            bandwidth : 2,   //< 12.5 / 25kHz
            _reserved : 4;   //< TODO: tone type (CTCSS/DCS/...)
};

struct m17Cfg {
    char    destination[10];    //< M17 destination
    uint8_t can       : 4,      //< Channel access number
            _reserved : 4;      //< TODO: encryption, ...
};

struct vfo {
    uint32_t power;         //< Tx power, in mW
    uint32_t rx_frequency;  //< RX Frequency, in Hz
    uint32_t tx_frequency;  //< TX Frequency, in Hz
    uint8_t mode;           //< Operating mode

    struct fmCfg fm;
#ifdef CONFIG_M17
    struct m17Cfg m17;
#endif
};
```
Given that the VFO channel is the "one channel to rule 'em all" because it includes all the operating modes at the same time, I think it is worth spending a bit of time to design all the data structures properly: then we'll use them as-is also for the codeplug channels.

Conceptually (and with more other fields I just omitted), a codeplug channel becomes:
```
struct channel {
    uint32_t power;         //< Tx power, in mW
    uint32_t rx_frequency;  //< RX Frequency, in Hz
    uint32_t tx_frequency;  //< TX Frequency, in Hz
    uint8_t mode;           //< Operating mode

    union {
        struct fmCfg fm;
        struct m17Cfg m17;
    };
};
```

I totally forgot about the need to have persistent VFO/memory mode and channel number. Now the question is: is there something else, in general, that we have to keep persistent across reboots? It seems like that at this point we are shifting towards more towards the concept of persistent state than just the tuner.

Apart from this, the approach is definitely good. I'm just wondering whether is good to have something like this
```
struct vfo {
    uint32_t txFrequency;
    [...]
};

struct tuner {
    uint32_t chIndex;
    uint8_t mode;    // VFO/MEM
    struct vfo vfo;
    struct channel channel;
};
```
or having the VFO data merged into the tuner structure
```
struct tuner {
    uint32_t chIndex;
    uint8_t vfo_mem;
    uint32_t txFrequency;
    [...]
    struct channel channel;
};
```

